t{―}{X})}^{2}].\n$$\n\nNote that:\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- the sample variance ${S}^{2}$ is an unbiased estimator of the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ and\n\n- ${\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2}={s}^{2}$ denotes an unbiased estimate of ${\\sigma }^{2}$ that's computed from the sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}.$\n\nWe're given that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{51}{({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}=24.5$ and $n=51.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2} & ={s}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{51-1}\\cdot 24.5 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}\\cdot 24.5 \\\\  & =0.49.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, an unbiased estimate of the population variance is ${\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2}=0.49.$ We take the square root to compute an unbiased estimate of the standard deviation. This gives\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\sigma }=\\sqrt{0.49}=0.7.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443479 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443476 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443479 #edb/ref 1069 "q-89000" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443479 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443479 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose the following data is sampled from a population. Compute an unbiased estimate of the population variance.\n\n$$\n19,\\,11,\\,20,\\,14,\\,16\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443479 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2f7daca4-074f-50e6-8f6a-0126ae82d27d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443479 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ Then, the sample variance ${S}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{S}^{2}=\\frac{1}{n-1}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}=\\frac{n}{n-1}[\\overset{―}{{X}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}].\n$$\n\nNote that:\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- the sample variance ${S}^{2}$ is an unbiased estimator of the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ and\n\n- ${\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2}={s}^{2}$ denotes the unbiased estimate of ${\\sigma }^{2}$ computed from the sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}.$\n\nFirst, we will compute the quantity\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe proceed as follows:\n\n- The mean of the data is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{5}(19+11+20+14+16) \\\\  & =\\frac{80}{5} \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The mean of the squared data is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{5}({19}^{2}+{11}^{2}+{20}^{2}+{14}^{2}+{16}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\,334}{5} \\\\  & =266.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2} & =266.8-{16}^{2} \\\\  & =10.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the sample variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2} & ={s}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{n}{n-1}[\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{5-1}\\cdot 10.8 \\\\  & =\\frac{54}{4} \\\\  & =13.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, an unbiased estimate of the population variance is $13.5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443480 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443469 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443480 #edb/ref 1069 "q-88962" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443480 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443480 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{26}$ is a random sample of size $n=26$ from a population, and \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}=5.\n$$\n\n \n\nCompute an unbiased estimate of the population variance." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443480 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4022c5eb-3ae0-5385-a3a9-5931c88f8e95" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443480 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with p